  "answer_text": "The complete quote is:\n\n> **\"...those owned by wealthy rulers.\"**\n\nThis quote is attributed to the legendary 14th-century North African scholar, historian, and philosopher **Ibn Khaldun**. It comes from his highly influential magnum opus, **The Muqaddimah** (written in 1377), which is widely considered one of the foundational texts of modern sociology, historiography, and economics.\n\n### Context and Meaning\nIn *The Muqaddimah*, Ibn Khaldun dedicates a specific section of Chapter 3 to the idea that **\"commercial activity on the part of the ruler is harmful to his subjects and ruinous to the tax revenue.\"** \n\nHe argued that when a government or an absolute ruler enters the marketplace as a competitor, it spells disaster for the economy for several reasons:\n\n1. **Unfair Competition and Coercion:** Rulers hold a monopoly on force. When a ruler wants to buy goods, sellers are forced to sell to them at artificially low prices out of fear or political obligation. Conversely, when the ruler wants to sell those goods, they can force the public to buy them at exorbitant prices. No ordinary merchant can compete against an entity that has the power to write the laws and control the army.\n2. **Destruction of the Private Sector:** Because private merchants cannot compete with the state's coercive advantages, they are driven out of business. When the private merchant class collapses, the overall flow of trade and production in the nation grinds to a halt.\n3. **Erosion of the Tax Base:** Ibn Khaldun pointed out that rulers who try to fund their empires through state-run business rather than tax revenues end up destroying their own tax base. As private business declines, tax yields plummet. To compensate, the state often increases taxes, further stifling what remains of the economy.\n\n### Why \"Responsible and Organized\" Merchants Win\nBy contrast, Ibn Khaldun believed that **private, organized merchants** must rely on discipline, efficiency, risk management, and genuine customer satisfaction to survive. Because they do not have the power of state coercion, they must build resilient, highly organized supply chains and practice strict financial stewardship. \n\nIn the long run, Ibn Khaldun asserted that the voluntary, organic market driven by professional merchants is far more sustainable, productive, and wealthy than any commercial enterprise run by a ruler with a treasury and an army. \n\n### Modern Significance\nThis quote is often cited by economists as one of the earliest defenses of **free-market capitalism and privatization**, predating Adam Smith's *The Wealth of Nations* by four centuries. It remains a timeless warning against \"state capitalism,\" government-backed monopolies, and the dangers of combining political authority with commercial enterprise.",
